# Clipper settings. Copy to .env (gitignored, never commit it): cp .env.example .env
# Every setting and its default: backend/app/core/config.py. compose.yml hands each container only what it needs:
# every service blanks the secrets it must not hold.
# Where a .env lives:
#   production     ~/clipper/.env on the VM, plus COMPOSE_FILE, CLIPPER_HOST and CLIPPER_ACME_EMAIL (docs/deploy.md
#                  section 1); compose.prod.yml sets APP_BASE_URL from CLIPPER_HOST
#   the dev site   ~/clipper-dev/.env on the VM, plus COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=clipper-dev and the staging COMPOSE_FILE
#                  (docs/deploy.md section 7)
#   development    a checkout's own .env, with throwaway secrets and none of the operator's values; run it under its
#                  own project (docker compose -p <name> ...), never with the Mac's .env (CLAUDE.md, "Do not")

# false: scheduled posts stay SCHEDULED and nothing reaches Instagram (the UI says "Publishing off").
# true: due posts are published as real Reels, each with its user's own Zernio key. They cannot be deleted
# through Zernio. Production and the dev site: true (the dev site's posts go out for real too). Development: false.
PUBLISHING_ENABLED=false

# Instagram's own music library (search and attach a catalog track to a post) through Zernio. It only works for
# Instagram accounts connected to Zernio with Facebook Login, so it is off; songs users upload need no switch.
# INSTAGRAM_CATALOG_MUSIC=false

# Where browsers open the app: sign-in cookies are Secure when it is https, every change must come from this
# origin, and alert and "Open in Clipper" links point here (Telegram buttons only when it is https). Development
# default: Vite's address; production and the dev site: https://CLIPPER_HOST (compose.prod.yml sets it).
# APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173

# The app's two secrets. On the VM deploy.sh adds both to each checkout's .env on its first run (never printed), so
# production and the dev site each have their own. Keep a copy of production's SECRETS_KEY in a password manager, as a
# backup (without it every user pastes their Zernio key and bot tokens again), and never put either in another machine's
# .env. Never set SECRETS_KEY in a .env that still holds the operator's ZERNIO_API_KEY or TELEGRAM_* (the Mac's): the
# next migrate (even `docker compose run --rm worker pytest`'s) imports production's key and bots into that database,
# for good. The VM's two .env files are the exceptions: production's still holds them from the multi-user release's
# one-shot import (2026-10-05) until that release is final, the dev site's holds the key on purpose (below). For a
# development stack without them:
#   echo "SECRETS_KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_')" >> .env
#   echo "BOT_SERVICE_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> .env
# SECRETS_KEY: Fernet key(s) sealing each user's Zernio key and bot tokens in the database (comma-separated to
# rotate: the first seals, any opens). Unset: no stored key opens, so nobody can save a key or publish.
# BOT_SERVICE_SECRET: the bot service's bearer, with which it acts as each bot's owner. Unset: no bots run.
# SECRETS_KEY=
# BOT_SERVICE_SECRET=

# Users. Anyone can sign up (username + password) until MAX_USERS users are enabled, the operator included (on the
# dev site, users copied from production count too). A new user may store USER_QUOTA_BYTES of clips and renders (the
# operator: no cap; `python -m app.cli set-quota <username> <GB|none>` changes it). Nobody can upload, import or
# render while the disk has less than MIN_FREE_BYTES free (on the VM both sites share one disk).
# MAX_USERS=15
# USER_QUOTA_BYTES=5368709120
# MIN_FREE_BYTES=3221225472

# User 1, the operator (read by the migrate service's `python -m app.cli bootstrap`, every other container gets
# them blanked). CLIPPER_USER renames it (default: clipper). CLIPPER_PASSWORD_HASH becomes its password while it
# has none: a bcrypt hash, base64-encoded so compose has no `$` to expand (docs/deploy.md section 1). The dev site's
# .env copies production's. On a fresh development database user 1 has no password: set one, or just sign up:
#   docker compose -p <name> exec api python -m app.cli set-password clipper
# CLIPPER_USER=
# CLIPPER_PASSWORD_HASH=

# Zernio keys and Telegram bots are per user: each pastes their own in Settings (bots: as many as they like). Operator
# only, one-shot: an install from before accounts existed has ZERNIO_API_KEY, TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN / TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID (the
# alerts bot) and _2 / _3 (interactive only) here. Once SECRETS_KEY is set, the migrate service imports them into user
# 1, once (users.env_imported_at); production did at the 2026-10-05 release and ignores them since, and they can go once
# that release is final (docs/deploy.md section 4). The dev site keeps the operator's ZERNIO_API_KEY: every refresh
# (staging-refresh.sh) imports it into user 1 again. Never TELEGRAM_* there (compose.staging.yml blanks them anyway): a
# bot token polled from two places breaks the real bot. Development installs leave all of them out.

# Other settings (defaults in config.py), rarely changed.
# ffmpeg threads per render; the worker runs two renders at once (the dev site's, one).
# FFMPEG_THREADS=2
# A Netscape-format cookies file for yt-dlp, at a path the worker container can read (it mounts ./data at /data);
# only the operator's link imports use it.
# YTDLP_COOKIES_FILE=
# IDEMPOTENCY_SALT: never set it here. Empty in production, so its Idempotency-Keys never change;
# compose.staging.yml gives the dev site's api its own ("staging:").
